-- title:
--   Base-point freeness of L ⊗ H^{⊗ n} for large n
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $X$ a scheme and $f \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a morphism, let $H$ be a sheaf of modules on $X$, let $N$ be a natural number, and let $\mathfrak{P}$ be a `ProjPresentation` of $H$ over $f$ of dimension $N$: that is, global sections $\sigma_0,\dots,\sigma_N \in \Gamma(H,\top)$ together with a morphism $\mathfrak{P}.\mathrm{toProj} \colon X \to \operatorname{Proj}$ of the homogeneous subalgebra of $R[x_0,\dots,x_N]$ whose composition with the structure morphism $\pi$ of projective space is $f$, such that for each $i$ and each open $V$ of $X$ contained in the preimage of the basic open $D_+(x_i)$, the map $g \mapsto g \cdot \sigma_i|_V$ from $\Gamma(X,V)$ to $\Gamma(H,V)$ is bijective, and such that over the preimage of $D_+(x_i)$ the pullback along $\mathfrak{P}.\mathrm{toProj}$ of the ratio $x_j/x_i$ carries $\sigma_i$ to $\sigma_j$. Assume $\mathfrak{P}.\mathrm{toProj}$ is an affine morphism, and let $L$ be a sheaf of modules on $X$ that is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ on which the pullback of $L$ along $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of $U$. Then there is an $n_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $n \ge n_0$ and every point $x$ of $X$ there exists a morphism $s \colon \mathbf{1} \to L \otimes H^{\otimes n}$ from the unit object, where $H^{\otimes n}$ is the $n$-fold tensor power defined by $H^{\otimes 0} = \mathbf{1}$ and $H^{\otimes (n+1)} = H^{\otimes n} \otimes H$, with $x$ outside the support of $\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}(s)$, the least ideal sheaf datum on $X$ whose ideal on each affine open $U$ contains the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ generated by the coefficients of $s$ over $U$. Note that $n_0$ is independent of the point $x$.
--
--   This is Serre's Theorem A in the form of base-point freeness: $H$ plays the role of the pullback of $\mathcal{O}(1)$ along an affine morphism to $\mathbb{P}^N_R$, hence of an ample line bundle, and the conclusion is that $L \otimes H^{\otimes n}$ is generated by global sections at every point once $n$ is large. It is used in the study of the relative group law on Jacobians with good reduction, in the argument deducing vanishing of a coefficient from the behaviour of Euler characteristics of tensor powers.
